# Sashimi design model -- the conceptual contract

Distilled from `sandboxes/sashimi/docs/design-overview.html` (Claude's
interpretation of the Unity design, author-reviewed 2026-07-06). That HTML
is the review record; THIS file is the durable contract future sessions
load. The divergence-resolution protocol (code vs docs vs Unity C#) lives
in `sandboxes/sashimi/CLAUDE.md`, "Design model and divergence protocol".

The authoritative slot-by-slot system list is the ordering table in
`sandboxes/sashimi/CLAUDE.md` -- this document never duplicates it; it
states the responsibilities and rules the table implements.

## 1. The tick model

- The simulation is an authoritative 60 Hz tick. Content times are
  authored in seconds and converted by the tick rate
  (ticks = seconds x 60), so design values stay human-readable and the
  tick rate stays a single constant.
- A second clock rides on top: the 4 Hz periodic tick (0.25 s), driving
  aura and poison damage. DoT cadence is a design constant, not a frame
  artifact. Both clocks first fire at tick 15.
- Prediction/rollback is DEPRECATED. Networked play has been abandoned.
  The Unity predicted chain and its once-per-authoritative-tick gates are
  artifacts of the networked era. The port's model -- one world where
  every tick is authoritative, one engine turn == one tick, every gate
  trivially true -- is the intended END-STATE of the design, not a
  simplification of it.

## 2. The pipeline as a responsibility contract

Each conceptual stage below states: its role (in the author's framing),
what systems in it MAY and MUST NOT do, the Unity group it maps from, and
the port phase it maps to. Between Unity's predicted groups sits an ECB
playback boundary: structural changes (entity create/destroy) are
deferred to the group end and become visible next group at the earliest,
usually next tick (see rule 3.3).

### Meta (BeginInitializationSimulationSystemGroup; conceptually MetaInitializationSimulationGroup)

Role: handle the events and functions that SURROUND the turn-based
simulation itself -- gathering input data, and handling transitions such
as starting and ending the game. Interfaces the game with everything
around it: possession, pause, game over.
- MAY: capture input, drive game flow (possess/pause/start/end), check
  victory/defeat (reads last tick's time).
- MUST NOT: simulate the turn itself or mutate in-turn gameplay state.
- Port: Init phase (rewind, app_start, game_time, hero_ai -- the headless
  input stand-in, bound by the observability contract: on-field reads
  only, no future knowledge).

### TurnInitialization (FirstSimulationSystemGroup; author: name could be better, leaving as is)

Role: BETWEEN-TURNS work and initialization of entities -- establish this
tick's time and finish initializing anything born last tick.
- MAY: advance the tick counter, set the 4 Hz periodic-clock flag, bake
  spawn-time stats/collision for entities created last tick.
- MUST NOT: make gameplay decisions or act on this tick's world.
- Port: Init phase (lands in game_time).

### Beginning-of-turn setup (WorldInitializationSystemGroup)

Role: beginning-of-turn concepts necessary so simulation of the turn can
occur -- ARM intents and effects before the world moves.
- MAY: run ability stage machines, fire weapons (spawn requests), mark
  timed despawns. Ready-checks here deliberately read LAST tick's spatial
  snapshot (one-tick staleness, in parity with the port's Reset phase).
- MUST NOT: move anything, or expect this tick's spatial state (it does
  not exist yet).
- Port: Reset phase (start_of_tick .. weapon_fire).

### Space, intent, movement (WorldSimulationSystemGroup)

Role: begin the turn's simulation with SPACE, INTENT, and MOVEMENT --
perception -> decision -> motion, strictly ordered -- before determining
the gameplay consequences. The spatial hash is rebuilt BEFORE movement,
so this tick's consumers see pre-move positions. Pinned chain:
SpatialHashing -> EnemyTargeting -> CreatureMoveDirection -> Move ->
GameplayChildOffsetSync.
- MAY: rebuild the proximity index, acquire targets, steer, integrate
  motion, sync child offsets, track the hero centroid (gameplay-relevant:
  it is the wave-spawn origin).
- MUST NOT: determine gameplay consequences (damage, statuses, pickups'
  gameplay effects) -- motion only.
- Port: Move phase (spatial_hash .. child_offset_sync; pickup_magnet
  lives here by the intended Move/Act split, see section 5).

### Determine all consequences (EffectApplicationSimulationSystemGroup)

Role: determine ALL the gameplay consequences that will happen this turn
-- contacts, damage buffers, statuses, triggers -- applied as EVENTS, never
final state, so order-of-operations issues cannot arise.
- MAY: detect trigger overlaps, route collision events, accumulate damage
  buffers (hero same-tick hits collapse to MAX, others sum -- rule 3.2),
  apply on-hit statuses, collect pickups.
- MUST NOT: write final state (HP subtraction, death, i-frame grants) --
  it emits events for resolution to consume.
- Port: Act phase (collision_push [retired no-op] .. pickup_collect).
  The port's damage_apply slot collapses apply + resolve into the same
  tick while keeping the aggregation-point semantics intact.

### Resolve all effects (EffectResolutionSimulationSystemGroup)

Role: resolve ALL effects, so order-of-operations issues do not affect
outcomes -- convert the aggregated events into state exactly ONCE: HP,
death, immunity, status ticks. ResolveDamage runs first; everything else
is after it.
- MAY: apply the net damage buffer, start i-frames, mark defeat, tick
  status durations (poison on the periodic clock), expire off-field
  windows.
- MUST NOT: emit new effect events for this tick, or perform irreversible
  structural consequences (births, drops, destruction -- those are turn
  end).
- Port: Act phase (status_effects, defeat, iframes).

### TurnEnd (FullyPredictedSimulationSystemGroup; conceptually TurnEndSimulationSystemsGroup -- plus FixedStepWorldSimulationSystemGroup)

Role: TURN END -- the irreversible consequences of the turn: births,
deaths, drops, destruction. The Unity group is a rollback-era artifact
(it existed to avoid re-simulating irreversible events during rollback);
with rollback deprecated it could merge into EffectResolution, though a
distinct turn-end group may be worth keeping regardless. FixedStep
(HeroLevelUpSystem: authoritative, irreversible, UI-coupled progression
outside the predicted chain) had the same goal by different means; with
rollback deprecated it conceptually folds into turn end too.
- MAY: run the wave script and delayed spawns, roll drops, respawn
  heroes, expire projectiles, destroy ghosts, level heroes up -- once per
  tick, irreversibly.
- MUST NOT: feed anything back into this tick (its outputs are next
  tick's inputs; new entities materialize at the boundary, rule 3.3).
- Port: Finalize phase (projectile_lifecycle .. history_capture; level_up
  hosts the FixedStep member; app_stop's end-of-tick game-over check ==
  Unity's start-of-next-tick check).

### Presentation (PresentationSimulationSystemGroup)

Role: sprites, HUD, audio -- out of simulation scope.
- MAY: read simulation state and render/play it.
- MUST NOT: mutate simulation state. The port's browser client owns this
  layer entirely; the engine stays headless.

## 3. Ordering principles -- normative rules

1. **Single-tick completeness.** A tick's causal chain completes inside
   the tick: intent -> motion -> contact -> effect events -> resolution
   -> consequences. Producers are ordered BEFORE their consumers. The
   canonical integration mistake is placing a producer after its
   consumer, which silently costs one frame of latency (e.g. creating
   spawn data after the system that consumes it).
2. **Two-phase effects.** EffectApplication determines all the
   consequences; EffectResolution resolves all the effects. The split
   exists precisely so that ordering within a turn CANNOT change
   outcomes: apply everything first, then convert the aggregate to state
   exactly once. Cross-source rules live at the aggregation point.
   Author-confirmed rationale for the aggregation rule: heroes become
   invulnerable for a short time when they take damage, enemies do not --
   so a hero's simultaneous same-tick hits collapse to the MAX
   (approximating one hit + immunity) while enemy damage sums.
3. **Structural changes at boundaries.** Creates and destroys are
   deferred to group/tick boundaries (Unity: ECB playback per predicted
   group; port: visible next tick). The spawn pipeline leans on this
   DELIBERATELY: wave entry -> warning entity -> creature materializes
   1.25 s later; one warning per tick shapes the wave front as a trickle.
4. **Once per authoritative tick -- surviving meaning: turn end.** With
   prediction/rollback deprecated, the principle's surviving meaning is
   that everything irreversible lives at turn end. Port equivalence: the
   Finalize phase of a fixed-step loop, where the gate is trivially true.
5. **Two clocks.** The 60 Hz tick and the 4 Hz periodic tick; DoT cadence
   is a design constant, not a frame artifact.

## 4. Integrating a new system -- decision procedure

1. Choose the stage by RESPONSIBILITY:
   - input / game flow -> Meta (Init)
   - between-turns / init-of-born -> TurnInitialization (Init)
   - arm intents and effects -> beginning-of-turn setup (Reset)
   - perceive / decide / move -> space-intent-movement (Move)
   - emit effect events -> determine-all-consequences (Act)
   - events -> state, exactly once -> resolve-all-effects (Act)
   - irreversible consequences -> TurnEnd (Finalize)
   - rendering / audio -> presentation (client, never the engine)
2. Then order WITHIN the stage by dataflow: whatever consumes your output
   this tick must run after you (rule 3.1; check the ordering table's
   "ordering-sensitive chains").
3. Times are authored in seconds and converted by the tick rate.
4. Config enters via singletons (GameConfig-style entities / app.yaml),
   never client-injected (root CLAUDE.md decision 13).

## 5. Known intent-vs-code notes

Places where shipped Unity code and author intent diverge; the full
author-reviewed flag list is in design-overview.html, section 4.

- **Heal-before-damage is a hack, not a rule.** The Unity PickupSystem
  comment declares itself a hack (a lost-update workaround forcing heal
  to commit before damage resolution). Author intent: apply all effects,
  resolve the net, clamp -- order must not matter. The port's arithmetic
  matches the intent (at full health, take 10 + heal 10 in the same tick
  yields 100); shipped Unity yields 90 at that edge -- an artifact of the
  hack, i.e. a Unity bug relative to intent, not a semantic to port.
- **Pickup movement fused with consumption is a confessed wart.** Unity's
  PickupSystem both moves pickups and applies their gameplay, and its own
  TODOs say those should be split so movement happens during world
  simulation (PickupSystem.cs). The port's split -- magnet movement in
  Move, collection in Act -- IS the intended design, not a deviation.

For everything else (crowd separation is steering, DamageThisTick is a
tick-keyed buffer, hero MAX aggregation is confirmed intent): the code is
authoritative and the rulings are recorded in design-overview.html.

## 6. References

- `sandboxes/sashimi/docs/design-overview.html` -- the author-reviewed
  interpretation this contract distills (group cards, flags, rulings).
- `sandboxes/sashimi/CLAUDE.md` -- the ordering table (authoritative slot
  list), port decisions, observability contract, divergence protocol.
- `sandboxes/sashimi/docs/fidelity-audit.md` -- system-by-system trace of
  the port against the Unity C#, with citations.
- `~/Dev/sashimi/Assets/Scripts/` -- the Unity C# spec for ported
  semantics (SystemGroups.cs defines the group order this model names).
